## 6. Why Do We Need Nested Loops?

We have two dimensions:

1. Rows represent customers.
2. Columns represent bank accounts.

The outer loop selects a customer.

The inner loop calculates the total wealth of that customer.

**Memory Shortcut:**

Outer Loop = Customer

Inner Loop = Bank Accounts

---

## 7. Time and Space Complexity

Let:

- `m` = Number of customers (rows)
- `n` = Number of bank accounts per customer (columns)

| Complexity | Value | Explanation |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(m × n) | Visits every element |
| Auxiliary Space | O(1) | Uses only two variables |

No additional array or data structure is created.

---

## 8. Common Mistakes

❌ Using `accounts[i]` instead of `accounts[i][j]` to access one balance.

❌ Initializing `sum` outside the outer loop.

❌ Comparing array rows instead of their calculated sums.

❌ Returning inside the loops before checking all customers.

❌ Returning `sum` instead of `largestSum`.

❌ Forgetting to compare each customer's wealth with the maximum.

